#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x$?\n\n1. The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n$, where $n$ is an integer\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n3. The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3567/Source/Images/203bd5e873deaa54322cd7fb4cb6610d.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n$. Therefore, statement I is false.\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $[MATH: (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).]$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11009" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 8$, then what is $\\csc(x-8\\pi)$?" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\csc x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\csc{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x &= \\csc (x + 2 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\csc (x - 8π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = 8\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\csc{(x-8\\pi)} = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11015" :question/problem "If $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, then find the value of $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\csc{x}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3567/Source/Images/8c39f3895fd26e9d53a573809cdb9ee3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\csc{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\csc{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\csc(-x) = -\\csc{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\csc{x}$, we have\n$\\csc (-x - 20π)|= \\csc (-x - 2π \\cdot 10); = \\csc (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\csc{x}$, we have\n$\\csc (-x)|=-\\csc x; =-\\frac{7}{2}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x - 20\\pi) = -\\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$"}]}
